WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.833
- Why do we put candles on birthday cake?

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.810
Hi, there, I'm Aria and I love birthdays.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.957
- Hi, I'm Ray and I love to bake cakes.

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:11.920
- And today, we're going to talk about

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.614
the history of birthday cake.

00:00:13.614 --> 00:00:17.697
(celebratory instrumental music)

00:00:19.465 --> 00:00:20.900
Happy birthday, Ray!

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.000
- This is weird, Aria.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.170
- So it's not your birthday?

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.003
- Yes.

00:00:24.003 --> 00:00:26.180
- Or because on a base level it's a little

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.590
odd that to celebrate you emerging from

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.440
your mother's womb you expect a

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.973
beautifully decorated
pastry to be set on fire.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.310
- Because it's not my birthday.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.970
- Great question, Ray,
how did birthday cakes

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.273
become the tradition that they are today?

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.393
I have a story for you.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.080
- You know it isn't my birthday.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.480
- [Aria] Apart from democracy and a

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.293
whole bunch of philosophers,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.480
we can also thank the ancient Greeks

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.353
for the first burning cakes.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.140
Greeks would make round cheesecakes

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.143
as offerings to the goddess
of the moon, Artemis.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.253
They then put candles on the cake,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.700
both because they thought fire could

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.570
send good intentions to the gods

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.313
and so the cakes would glow like the moon.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.830
Isn't that such a nice thought?

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.823
- It's nice, seems like a lot of work.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.180
You know, some religions, they
just light a candle on fire.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.800
You know, be like, "Cool, we're done."

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.810
- I feel like it's a lot of effort

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.300
to get a prayer out, honestly.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.133
- Little bit.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:17.870
- [Aria] And while that is believed to be

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.710
the birth of the flaming cake,

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.163
it wasn't to celebrate anyone's birthday.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.410
One reason was because, well,

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.193
no one knew when anyone was born.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.330
The practice of recording birthdays

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.820
wouldn't become popular
until the Christian church

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.403
began doing so in the medieval period.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.560
The tradition of throwing birthday parties

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.453
for children may date back
to Germany in the 1400s.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.593
They were called Kinderfests
or, literally, children party.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
Germans believed children
were particularly susceptible

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.010
to evil spirits on their
birthdays and that candles

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.000
on the cake would transport
their wishes to God.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.570
Families and friends kept
the candles lit all day,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.423
keeping watch until
after the evening meal,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.193
when the cake would be served.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.040
- Imagine being, like, just
a horrible older sibling

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.357
and you just, like, "I
want my younger sibling

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:08.107
"to just have a bad
birthday, I'm gonna make sure

00:02:08.107 --> 00:02:10.150
"that their birthday
candle's gonna be off."

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.133
- Man, that's sadistic.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.330
Foreshadowing the amount of attention

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.943
birthdays demand today,

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:19.320
in 1746 German Count Ludwig von Zinzendorf

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.100
celebrated his birthday with a giant cake

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.103
on a table in the shape
of an L for Ludwig.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.820
The cake had holes bored
into it according to

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.793
the person's age and to
place the candles into.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.630
Oh, and he also had his name spelled out

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.803
in two-foot-tall golden letters.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.103
How basic.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.880
- I feel like your
measurements are a little off.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.550
- Yeah, I wouldn't say this is to scale,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.040
but I think if you squint a little bit

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.240
it can look pretty big.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.733
Squint, use your imagination.

00:02:48.977 --> 00:02:50.260
If I look at it through my fingers,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.480
it looks pretty big to me.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.550
This tradition made its way to America

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.890
when large numbers of Germans emigrated

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.683
to Pennsylvania during
the colonial period.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.270
American women became more prolific bakers

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.870
than their European counterparts

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.240
due to the relative absence of bakeries

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.053
and abundance of oven fuel; aka, trees.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.720
The cakes they were making, however,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.303
were still pretty lame
by today's standards.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.663
Fruit cakes and yeasty, rich cakes, gross.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.180
What we think of cake couldn't emerge

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.480
until British chemist Alfred Bird combined

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.093
sodium bicarbonate and cream of tartar.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.573
Baking powder, well an
early version anyway.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.680
Finally, cakes could rise
without yeast and achieve

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.400
a level of light fluffiness
that had previously

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.183
been extremely difficult
and time-consuming.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.010
- This is definitely something closer to

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.773
what I would bake currently.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.673
It's lighter and it's
less of a bread loaf.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:47.543
Less hockey puck-like.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:52.870
Yeah, pretty different.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.580
- [Aria] Not long after, more festive

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.200
decorations and frostings became popular.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.630
Back then, cookbooks included recipes for

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:03.393
colored frostings made from
parsley and beets, yum.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.100
We should try it, I'm gonna try it though.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.000
Let's just try it.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.033
That's actually really good, wow.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.860
- It is, I'm not mad at that.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.460
- That tasted good so who knows.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.533
- It's a chunky-looking green.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.690
I don't know how I'm gonna feel.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.280
- Whoa, whoa, no, that
really tastes like parsley.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.190
Yeah, the texture of the
parsley, you can feel.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.603
- It's like a wheat grass
shot poured into frosting.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.090
- That's messed up, that's
messed up, that's sadistic.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.340
- That one we could burn.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.670
- [Aria] Light, fluffy cakes with candles

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.343
and colored frosting were
given out on birthdays.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.350
Sounds like the birthday cake
we all know and love, right?

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.083
Not quite yet.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.220
While it was popular to
have writing on cakes

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.770
in the late 1800s they would say things

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.387
like the person's name along with,

00:04:57.387 --> 00:05:00.403
"Many happy returns of the day," catchy.

00:05:00.618 --> 00:05:01.540
- It's a lot to write on a cake.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.210
- Yeah, so too much effort for me.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.630
- So they didn't write happy birthday.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:05.890
- No, no, no Happy Birthday.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.320
You see, Happy Birthday
didn't appear on cakes

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.650
until after 1910 with the popularization

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.113
of the song Happy Birthday to You.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.840
- So we've only been saying Happy Birthday

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.390
for about a hundred years right now?

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:18.290
- It's crazy, isn't it?

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:19.617
Before then, you'd just go around and be,

00:05:19.617 --> 00:05:21.460
"Happy returns to you on the day."

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.030
- Everything seemed like a lot
more effort back in the day.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:24.863
- A lot more effort.

00:05:24.863 --> 00:05:26.640
There were gods involved,
there was praying,

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.680
there were giant Ls, it's
just too much effort.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.260
Pre-made cake mixes were
available starting around 1930,

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:37.133
but wouldn't become more popular
until after World War II.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.360
And the late 1930s was also when Carvel

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.263
invented the ice cream cake.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.530
The gates of cake innovation were not

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.650
just open but shattered.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.000
- I love a good ice cream cake.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.610
I'd never make one but I love
eating a good ice cream cake.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:51.463
- Why won't you make an ice cream cake?

00:05:51.463 --> 00:05:52.993
- It's too much work, man.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:54.700
- What's your favorite
flavor of ice cream, Ray?

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.173
- Mint or cookies and cream.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.430
- Two of my least favorite flavors.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.890
- That's why we can never be together.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:03.073
- [Aria] Today, birthday cakes
are more elaborate than ever.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:08.090
None more so than a 2015
six-foot-long, 1000-pound behemoth.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.780
Purchased by an unnamed buyer
for his daughter's birthday,

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.373
the cake featured over 4000 diamonds.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:19.563
Final price, 75 million dollars.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.910
- It's 2015 and a guy decides
to buy a birthday cake

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.253
and wants to remain anonymous?

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.250
You know that that's gonna
end up on Instagram somewhere.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.200
The cake probably had it's own hashtag.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.033
- What would the hashtag be?
75 million dollar cake cake?

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.240
- Hashtag diamonds on my cake.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.690
- I like that, that's
great, that's awesome, man.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:41.723
Good job.

00:06:41.723 --> 00:06:43.823
So where does that leave us today?

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.623
- Funny you should ask.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.110
Because I kinda see them more like this.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:49.750
When I think of birthday cakes I think of

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.680
sprinkles, bright colors, and things that

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.180
just make you wanna celebrate.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.860
It is a modern rainbow-drip birthday cake.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.430
This is actually a giant cake pop

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:01.840
made to look like an ice cream cone.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:02.930
That is incredible, Ray.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.493
That is incredible.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.000
This is not made from parsley, right?

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.920
- That is not made from parsley but

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:10.900
modern cake.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.670
- [Aria] For those of
us who can only dream

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.250
of the ultimate birthday cake,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.200
multiple television and
internet programs showcase

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.360
fancy, sculptural, and
complex birthday cakes.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.100
As long as people keep having birthdays,

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.963
the birthday cake will
continue to adapt and evolve.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.853
Oh, wait, wait.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.443
In a study actually published in 2017,

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.810
scientists found that
blowing out birthday candles

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.400
can increase the chances
of bacteria being found

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.443
in icing by about 1400 percent.

00:07:38.443 --> 00:07:39.593
- So what do we do now?

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.583
- Not my problem.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.030
Happy birthday!

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:46.863
- It's still not my birthday.

00:07:47.148 --> 00:07:48.742
♪ It's your birthday ♪

00:07:48.742 --> 00:07:50.604
♪ Happy birthday ♪

00:07:50.604 --> 00:07:51.962
♪ Lots of happiness ♪

00:07:51.962 --> 00:07:54.189
♪ Good luck, too ♪

00:07:54.189 --> 00:07:57.904
♪ Congratulations on your birthday ♪

00:07:57.904 --> 00:08:01.571
♪ Many happy returns to you ♪

